{
  "chapter": "retrieval/chunking",
  "tier": "build",
  "notes": [
    "Chunking sets the ceiling for everything downstream. No embedding model finds a fact that",
    "was split across two chunks, and no reranker repairs a clause severed from its exception.",
    "Small chunks retrieve better and large chunks answer better. Parent/child dissolves the",
    "tension: index the small unit, return the section it came from.",
    "Parents are the document's own sections, so a heading always starts a new one. The",
    "strategy governs only how children are packed.",
    "A chunk must be answerable in isolation. A block listed in neverStartsAChunk may never",
    "begin one, so a child deliberately runs over its cap rather than cut a rule from its",
    "exception or an item from its list.",
    "Every chunk carries its heading trail, its document id and its version, because the",
    "citation the acceptance spec requires is only possible if chunking made it possible.",
    "Token counts are given per block, so the packing is exact and identical in both languages."
  ],
  "config": {
    "maxChildTokens": 100,
    "neverStartsAChunk": ["exception", "list-item"],
    "strategy": "structural"
  },
